        {
          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01PN7uFeHc6XUq5hQyeS7HTp",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022: \u0022concepts/rag-marketing-claims.md\u0022, \u0022content\u0022: \u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: RAG Marketing Claims vs. Reality\\ndescription: Critical analysis of common marketing claims about retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), particularly the claim that it eliminates hallucination entirely.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/RAG\\n  - concept/AI-Marketing\\n  - concept/Hallucination\\n  - source-type/synthesis\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\\u0022Single authoritative source on the specific claim (marketing accuracy); general RAG properties are well-established but the marketing critique itself is narrowly sourced.\\\u0022\\n---\\n\\n## The Hallucination Elimination Claim\\n\\nMarketing materials for retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) products frequently claim that RAG \\\u0022eliminates hallucination entirely.\\\u0022 This claim is **not accurate**.\\n\\n## Why the Claim Is Misleading\\n\\nRAG does reduce hallucination compared to a pure parametric language model by grounding responses in retrieved documents. However, this improvement has a strict ceiling: **retrieval quality caps answer quality**. A generator cannot produce well-grounded answers from irrelevant or incomplete retrieved context.\\n\\n### Three Failure Modes Remain\\n\\n1. **Retrieval Failure** \u2014 The retriever fails to find relevant documents. When documents are absent or ranked poorly, the generator receives no good context to condition on.\\n\\n2. **Passage Relevance Mismatch** \u2014 The retriever returns documents that appear topically related but do not contain the specific information needed to answer the query accurately.\\n\\n3. **Generator Inference Error** \u2014 Even with relevant context present, the generator may misinterpret, selectively ignore, or inconsistently synthesize the retrieved information.\\n\\nAll three occur in production systems. RAG reduces but does not eliminate hallucination.\\n\\n## Marketing Practice\\n\\nVendors claiming \\\u0022eliminates hallucination\\\u0022 typically highlight only the most optimistic use case (high-quality retrieval over well-curated documents) while omitting or downplaying the contingency on retrieval quality. This is a common pattern in RAG product marketing.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: [[sources/steering-adoption-sample-01]]\\n\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
      "assistant_text": "Good\u2014the wiki is minimal and does not yet contain RAG-related content. According to my task framing, I should focus exclusively on the **marketing-claims accuracy angle** for RAG and ignore general RAG architecture. The source makes a factual claim about marketing accuracy (that \u0022RAG eliminates hallucination entirely\u0022 is not accurate), and this should become a page that specifically addresses this misconception.\n\nI\u0027ll create a new concept page specifically about RAG marketing accuracy claims:",
