          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01C5KKj6t5p1r8pffThHqQhh",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022: \u0022sources/steering-adoption-sample-01.md\u0022, \u0022content\u0022: \u0022---\\ntype: Source summary\\ntitle: Steering Adoption \u2014 Sample 01 (RAG Marketing Claims)\\ndescription: Evaluation sample examining the accuracy of marketing claims about RAG\u0027s ability to eliminate hallucination.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - source-type/evaluation\\n  - concept/RAG\\n  - concept/AI-Marketing\\nconfidence: high\\nconfidence_reason: \\\u0022Direct evaluation artifact; author analysis is authoritative within scope of marketing-claims accuracy assessment.\\\u0022\\nresource: eval://steering-adoption/sample-01\\n---\\n\\n## Summary\\n\\nThis evaluation sample critiques common marketing claims about retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), specifically the assertion that RAG \\\u0022eliminates hallucination entirely.\\\u0022 The sample argues this claim is inaccurate because retrieval quality imposes a hard ceiling on answer quality: a generator cannot produce well-grounded answers from irrelevant context.\\n\\n## Key Claims\\n\\n- RAG does reduce hallucination compared to parametric-only models by grounding responses in retrieved documents.\\n- However, this improvement is bounded by retrieval quality.\\n- Marketing materials often overstate RAG\u0027s hallucination-reduction capabilities.\\n- The claim \\\u0022eliminates hallucination entirely\\\u0022 is not supported by the actual constraints of RAG systems.\\n\\n## Scope Note\\n\\nThis is an evaluation sample focused on **marketing-claims accuracy**, not a comprehensive treatment of RAG architecture, retrieval strategies, or generation techniques.\\n\\n## Related Pages\\n\\n- [[concepts/rag-marketing-claims]] \u2014 Full analysis of RAG marketing accuracy claims in the wiki.\\n\\n\u0022}"
